<setup>

  <function name="f">tan(x)</function>
  <derivative name="fP">$f</derivative>

  <function name="T">sin(x)</function>
  <derivative name="TP">$T</derivative>

  <function name="B">cos(x)</function>
  <derivative name="BP">$B</derivative>

  <boolean name="correctQR">$bot=$B and $Dtop=$TP and $top=$T and $Dbot=$BP and $bot2=$B</boolean>
  <boolean name="correctFinal">$fPfinal=sec^2(x) or $fPfinal=(sec(x))^2 or $fPfinal=sec(x)^2</boolean>

</setup>

<p>
    Consider the function <m>f(x) = \tan(x)</m>, and remember that
    <me>\tan(x) = \frac{\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}.</me>

    <ol marker="a." >
      <li >
        <p >
          Recall that <m>\tan(x)</m> is undefined at any value of <m>x</m> for which <m>\cos(x) = 0</m>. Therefore, the domain of <m>f</m> is all real numbers <m>x</m> such that
        </p>
         <answer>
          <choiceInput>
            <choice><m> x \neq \dots \, , \, -\frac{\pi}{2}, \, -\frac{\pi}{4}, \, 0, \, \frac{\pi}{4}, \, \frac{\pi}{2}, \, ...</m></choice>

            <choice credit="1"><m>x \neq \dots , \, -\frac{3\pi}{2}, \, -\frac{\pi}{2}, \, \frac{\pi}{2}, \, \frac{3\pi}{2}, \, ...</m></choice>

            <choice><m>x \neq \dots \, , \, -\pi, \, -\frac{\pi}{2}, \, 0, \, \frac{\pi}{2}, \, \pi, \, ...</m></choice>

            <choice><m>x \neq \dots \, , \, -2\pi, \, -\pi, \, 0, \, \pi, \, 2\pi, \, ...</m></choice>
          </choiceInput>
         </answer>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Apply the quotient rule to <m>f(x) = \frac{\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}</m> in order to fill in the following blanks in the structure of <m>f'(x)</m>:
        </p>
        <sideBySide widths="15% 82%">
          <p>
              <p>
                <m>\hspace{0.1in}</m>
              </p>
              <p>
                <m>f'(x) = </m>
              </p>
              <p>
                <m>\hspace{0.1in}</m>
              </p>
          </p>
          <p>
            <stack>
              <answer name="bot"><award>$B</award></answer><m>\cdot</m><answer name="Dtop"><award>$TP</award></answer><m>-</m><answer name="top"><award>$T</award></answer><m>\cdot</m><answer name="Dbot"><award>$BP</award></answer>
            </stack>
            <stack>
              <m>\overline{\hspace{4in}}</m>
            </stack>
            <stack>
              <m>\hspace{1.2in}\Big(</m> <mathInput name="bot2"/><m>\Big)^2</m> <answer forceSmallCheckWorkButton><award><when>$bot2=$B</when></award></answer>
            </stack>
          </p>
        </sideBySide>

      </li>

      <li >
        <p >
          The Fundamental Trigonometric Identity states that
          <p>
            <me>
              \sin^2(x)+\cos^2(x) = 1
            </me>
          </p>

        </p>
        <conditionalContent>
          <case condition="$correctQR = false">
            <p>
              (<em>A prompt will appear here once part (b) is answered correctly.</em>)
            </p>
          </case>
          <case condition="$correctQR = true">
            <p>
              Use the Fundamental Trigonometric Identity to generate a simpler form for <m>f'(x)</m> in the provided space below:
            </p>
            <p>
              <m>
                f'(x) = \frac{\cos(x) \cos(x) + \sin(x) \sin(x)}{\cos^2(x)} =
              </m> <mathInput name="dq_simp" minWidth="50" prefill="/(cos(x))^2"></mathInput>
      <answer symbolicEquality><award><when>$dq_simp =
        (1)/(cos(x))^2
      </when></award></answer>
            </p>
         </case>
<!--
<answer name= "PA_2-3-1_ans2" matchPartial symbolicEquality><award> <when> $expandedp=2t^5+8t^3 or $expandedp=8t^3+2t^5 </when></award> </answer>
-->
      </conditionalContent>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Recall that <m>\sec(x) = \frac{1}{\cos(x)}</m>.
          How can we now express <m>f'(x)</m> in an even simpler way in terms of the secant function?
          <p>
            <m>
              f'(x) =
            </m>
            <mathInput name="fPfinal" />
            <answer name="fPsimplest" symbolicEquality>
              <award><when>$fPfinal=sec^2(x) or $fPfinal=(sec(x))^2 or $fPfinal=sec(x)^2</when></award>
            </answer>
          </p>
          <conditionalContent>
          <case condition="$correctFinal= false">
            <p>

            </p>
          </case>
          <case condition="$correctFinal = true">
            <p>
              You've now correctly found the simplest form of the derivative of the tangent function:
              <me>
                f'(x) = \frac{d}{dx}[\tan(x)] = \sec^2(x).
              </me>
            </p>
         </case>
      </conditionalContent>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Because <m>\sec(x) = \frac{1}{\cos(x)}</m>, it follows that <m>f'(x)</m> is defined for all real numbers <m>x</m> such that
              <answer>
                <choiceInput>
                  <choice><m>x \neq \dots \, , \, -\frac{\pi}{2}, \, -\frac{\pi}{4}, \, 0, \, \frac{\pi}{4}, \, \frac{\pi}{2}, \, ...</m></choice>

                  <choice credit="1"><m>x \neq \dots , \, -\frac{3\pi}{2}, \, -\frac{\pi}{2}, \, \frac{\pi}{2}, \, \frac{3\pi}{2}, \, ...</m></choice>

                  <choice><m>x \neq \dots \, , \, -\pi, \, -\frac{\pi}{2}, \, 0, \, \frac{\pi}{2}, \, \pi, \, ...</m></choice>

                  <choice><m>x \neq \dots \, , \, -2\pi, \, -\pi, \, 0, \, \pi, \, 2\pi, \, ...</m></choice>
                </choiceInput>
              </answer>
          In particular, we notice that the domain of <m>f'(x)</m> is <answer>
                <choiceInput inline>
                  <choice>?</choice>
                  <choice>different from</choice>
                  <choice credit="1">the same as</choice>
                  <choice>unrelated to</choice>
                </choiceInput>
              </answer> the domain of <m>f</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>